<Guide
  title="Chiropractor SEO in Dallas–Fort Worth: Google, Maps & AI Search — Azoth & Co."
  description="How patients in McKinney, Frisco, Plano and across DFW choose a chiropractor now, and the fixes that move an owner-led practice into the map pack and AI answers. Start with a $2,500 Visibility Audit."
  label="Chiropractors"
  heading="Search visibility for chiropractors in Dallas–Fort Worth."
  sub="For chiropractors who own their practice. A patient in pain books one of the first offices she sees. Here is how to be one of them."
  published="2026-11-13"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Someone who woke up with a stiff neck in Plano, or hurt her back lifting boxes in McKinney, searches “chiropractor
    near me” and looks at the map. She opens two or three profiles, reads the newest reviews, checks whether she can be
    seen today and calls. More of them now ask ChatGPT or read Google’s AI answer first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chiropractic searches are local and often urgent. The office that is close, open and easy to book usually wins.
    Here is where most practices lose patients, and what fixes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where chiropractors lose patients</h2>
  <h3>A profile that says too little</h3>
  <p>
    “Chiropractor” as the primary category, with secondary categories only for work you really do. List each service
    on the profile: adjustments, sports care, prenatal care, and so on. Keep hours exact, including early and late
    slots. Patients in pain filter by who is open now.
  </p>
  <h3>Pages for the problem, not just the profession</h3>
  <p>
    Patients search “lower back pain Frisco”, “sciatica relief Allen”, “sports chiropractor Southlake”. A single services
    page ranks for none of those. Write a page for each condition and service you see often: what it feels like, what a
    first visit involves, how you work, and the towns you serve.
  </p>
  <h3>Cost and insurance left to a phone call</h3>
  <p>
    A patient comparing two offices books the one that answers her questions first. Say which plans you accept, whether
    you offer cash pricing, and what a first visit involves and costs.
  </p>
  <h3>Reviews that stopped</h3>
  <p>
    Patients read the newest reviews first. Reviews from two years ago lose to a steady flow from this season. Ask after
    a visit that went well, with a direct link, and reply to every review.
  </p>
  <h3>No same-day booking</h3>
  <p>
    A patient in pain wants to know she can be seen today. A booking link on the profile and every page, and a clear
    note on same-day or walk-in visits if you offer them, catches her before she calls the next office.
  </p>
  <h3>Nothing for AI to quote</h3>
  <p>
    AI assistants recommend practices they can confirm across sources. Clear condition and service pages, consistent
    listings and recent, specific reviews are what they read. Our guide to <a href="/visibility/ai-search/">AI
    search</a> covers it in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Chiropractic rules to respect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Do not promise a cure, a result or a set number of visits. Describe the care and who it suits.</li>
    <li>Keep patient details out of review replies. Thank them; do not confirm they are a patient.</li>
    <li>Keep advertised prices and offers accurate, with any conditions stated plainly.</li>
    <li>Never offer discounts or gifts in exchange for reviews.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the Visibility Audit covers for a chiropractic practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your map position for your top services and conditions, across the towns you draw from</li>
    <li>Your Google profile against the three practices that show up instead of you</li>
    <li>Condition and service pages, speed on a phone and booking flow</li>
    <li>What ChatGPT, Perplexity and Google’s AI answer say when a patient asks for a chiropractor in your town</li>
    <li>A ranked fix list with 90-day targets</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We take one practice per town. If yours is not already taken, <a href="/visibility/#audit">the Audit</a> is where
    it starts. Or check yourself first with the free <a href="/visibility/scorecard/">Scorecard</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
